Picture \par
\par
When we think of twins, we usually picture two people so similar that they would be almost impossible to tell apart. We imagine siblings who look the same and sound the same. So, when we read Webster\rquote s The Duchess of Malfi and find out that the titular character and Ferdinand are twins, it comes as a shock. With their troubled relationship that begins in disagreement and ends in murder, the Duchess and Ferdinand differ greatly from our expectations of what twins are like. \par
\par
Webster does not even let us know that two of his main characters are twins until the play is almost over. It is only when the Duchess is dead that Ferdinand tells us that \lquote She and I were twins\rquote  (4.2.259). Ferdinand is not the most straightforward of characters, and the timing of this revelation makes him seem even more complicated. Ferdinand has already been mistaken for the Cardinal\rquote s twin in the very first act of the play, so why wasn\rquote t his real twin relationship revealed while the Duchess was still alive? Why is it only mentioned when she has been murdered on Ferdinand\rquote s orders? \par
\par
When the audience realises that these two characters were twins, Ferdinand\rquote s actions become even more shocking. That he could order the execution of the person who shared a womb and a birthday with him is appalling. It emphasises his cruelty and callousness. But the knowledge that Ferdinand is the Duchess\rquote  twin has other effects, too. At the time that The Duchess of Malfi was written, there were certain medical beliefs surrounding twins. Understanding these ideas helps us to comprehend fully the relationship between Ferdinand and his sister. \par
\par
Twins in Webster\rquote s time \par
\par
When Webster wrote The Duchess of Malfi, physicians and midwives thought that male and female babies had to spend different amounts of time in the womb if they were to develop fully. In Helkiah Crooke\rquote s 1615 medical work Mikrokosmographia, for example, the author explains that \lquote the male and the female are not distinguished in the same time\rquote . The word \lquote distinguished\rquote  here means \lquote developed\rquote . So, Crooke is highlighting how male and female babies were not believed to grow at the same rate. This meant that when twins of different sexes came along, like the Duchess and Ferdinand, one of them was going to be less developed than the other. \par
\par
When Ferdinand admits that he and the Duchess are twins, he also adds that he was the younger one: \lquote And should I die this instant, I had lived / Her time to a minute\rquote  (4.2.260\f1\endash 61). The Duchess has only just been strangled, so Ferdinand is explaining that his twin was born only a short time before him. From this comment, people who saw or read the play during the 1600s would believe that it was the Duchess who developed fully as a child. As the older twin, the Duchess would be seen as stronger than Ferdinand. It was understood that the younger twin relied on the older one to help them to leave the womb and be born. Jacques Guillemeau\rquote s 1612 midwifery book, Child-birth or, The happy deliuerie of women, for example, states that the second twin can be born because \lquote the way hath beene prepared, and made ready by the former\rquote  or older twin. The audience would therefore believe that Ferdinand only existed in the first place because he had depended upon the Duchess, and that he would be weaker than his twin and more likely than her to become ill. \par
\par
\lquote Superior\rquote  or \lquote inferior\rquote ? \par
\par
So how do these seventeenth-century ideas about twins relate to the adult Ferdinand? The beliefs suggest a kind of \lquote backstory\rquote  for the relationship between Ferdinand and the Duchess. Since men had far more opportunities than women during the Renaissance, Webster presented Ferdinand as a character who felt that he was superior to the Duchess. But he was born after his twin, so the audience would know that he was so weak at birth that, without her help, he would not have lived. Ferdinand, therefore, could not be certain that he was the \lquote superior\rquote  twin. Other people would know these facts and make comparisons between the Duchess and Ferdinand too. \par
\par
From their very first hours, the relationship between the twins has never been as clear-cut as it should have been. The Duchess and Ferdinand both have claims to be the \lquote superior\rquote  twin, but each can also be seen as the \lquote inferior\rquote  one too. Ferdinand has never enjoyed the superiority that he feels should have been his, and he seems to blame the Duchess for it. \par
\par
Ferdinand\rquote s motivations \par
\par
Ferdinand\rquote s decision to have the Duchess executed by strangling is very significant. Her murder takes place in a dark, enclosed room, which might be read as symbolising the womb, and she is strangled with a cord, which could represent the umbilical cord of birth. When these interpretations come together, they suggest that Ferdinand organised the Duchess\rquote  murder in order to play out a fantasy that she had been strangled in the womb before birth. If the Duchess had died before birth, Ferdinand would never have had to be compared to her, and found lacking. There would have been no question over who the \lquote superior\rquote  or \lquote inferior\rquote  twin was. He would not have been seen as weak, and no one would have believed that he had ever had to rely upon his twin. \par
\par
One of Ferdinand\rquote s motivations for murdering the Duchess, then, is directly connected to the fact that he is her twin. When he asks Bosola \lquote Is she dead?\rquote  (4.2.249), he receives the reply \lquote She is what / You\rquote d have her\rquote  (4.2.249\endash 50), which suggests that Bosola was following Ferdinand\rquote s orders to kill her in a certain place and manner. Ferdinand seems to have given such specific orders because he wanted to imagine that he was born alone. The murder of the Duchess would therefore be his way of displaying that he is greater than the person he has been compared to for all of his life. By imagining that she never lived at all, he is offering evidence of his superior strength, as well as his independence. \par
\par
This is why Ferdinand admits that \lquote She and I were twins\rquote  when he does. Coming so soon after the Duchess\rquote  death, the comment is both an explanation for his actions and a way of celebrating the idea that he is not as dependent upon her as people would expect. The audience is soon shown that Ferdinand does not die at \lquote this instant\rquote , and so it has to acknowledge that he will actually outlive his twin. It is a triumphant moment for Ferdinand, and an uncomfortable one for the audience, particularly a seventeenth-century one. \par
\par
The flaw in Ferdinand\rquote s thinking \par
\par
As much as Ferdinand wants to imagine that the Duchess had died before she was born, or that he does not need to rely on her, he can never fully deny his connection to his twin. We can see evidence of this reading almost immediately after the Duchess has been murdered, when Ferdinand cannot decide whether he wants to look at her or not. He orders \lquote cover her face\rquote , then soon asks \lquote Let me see her face again\rquote . It seems that Ferdinand is trying to assert his independence by not looking at his twin, but his order to see her once more suggests that he still feels closely connected to her. \par
\par
Then there is the moment when Ferdinand rounds on Bosola, accusing him of misunderstanding his orders. He laments \lquote I bade thee, when I was distracted of my wits, / Go kill my dearest friend, and thou hast done\rquote t\rquote  (4.2.271\endash 72). Bosola and the audience know very well that Ferdinand never once acted like the Duchess was his \lquote dearest friend\rquote , but it is still important that this phrase is used. Ferdinand uses the word \lquote friend\rquote  to emphasise that the Duchess was both his relative and a person that he was fond of. He therefore suggests that he has lost somebody who was irreplaceable for two reasons: first, because the Duchess was his twin, and second, because he valued her company and advice very much. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Ferdinand does not only exaggerate how he felt about the Duchess when she was alive \emdash  he also seems to lie about why he had her murdered in the first place. He declares \lquote I must confess, I had a hope, / Had she continued widow, to have gained / An infinite mass of treasure by her death\rquote  (2.2.275\endash 77). Ferdinand tries to suggest that he was motivated by greed when he ordered the execution of the Duchess, but this explanation is not wholly convincing. As a wealthy Duke who is never shown to be in financial trouble at any point of the play, Ferdinand\rquote s avaricious attitude seems misplaced. Even his use of the word \lquote hope\rquote  is unconvincing, because it does not suggest a real need or desire for the Duchess\rquote  wealth. If Ferdinand was so desperate to prove that he was greater than his twin sister that he had her murdered, then why does he try to suggest that he adored her, but ordered her execution because he envied her wealth? The most obvious answer is that Ferdinand is panicking over the consequences of what he has done. It also seems, though, that he fabricates these stories because he feels that no one else could possibly understand his true feelings and motivations. \par
\par
Life without the Duchess \par
\par
For the rest of the play, Ferdinand is horrified at the reality of existence without the Duchess. He is plagued by a \lquote shadow\rquote  (5.2.35) that he cannot escape, which seems to symbolise the Duchess. This idea is also supported by the way that he decides to rid himself of the shadow by trying to \lquote throttle\rquote  (5.2.38) it, because the action echoes his earlier orders to strangle the Duchess. Her murder also seems to weigh on his mind, even when he is alone, as he remarks to himself that \lquote strangling is a very quiet death\rquote  (5.4.33). \par
\par
Even his dying words are concerned with his twin: \lquote My sister! O my sister! There\rquote s the cause on\rquote t: / Whether we fall by ambition, blood, or lust, / Like diamonds, we are cut with our own dust\rquote  (5.5.70\endash 72). He is most obviously suggesting that if the Duchess had not fallen in love with Antonio, none of the play\rquote s awful events would have happened. But he is also calling her the \lquote cause\rquote  because, as she was his elder twin, her presence made him feel inadequate. He believed that he could live without her, but found that he could not. So, she has thwarted his \lquote ambition\rquote  to prove that he is superior. Since the Duchess is dead, Ferdinand believes that he must die, too. The image of the \lquote diamonds\rquote  in Ferdinand\rquote s last line therefore serves as an analogy for their relationship: the surfaces of these precious jewels are equally strong, so whenever two diamonds meet, they both \lquote cut\rquote  each other. In the same way, Ferdinand and the Duchess are so connected that whatever happens to one twin must happen to the other, too. Just as the meeting of diamonds produces \lquote dust\rquote , so too do the shared experiences of the Duchess and Ferdinand create painful, harmful emotions which lead to their deaths. \par
\par
By understanding that Webster\rquote s original audience and readers would have viewed Ferdinand differently once they knew that he was the Duchess\rquote  twin, we gain a deeper insight into his motivations for murdering her. We may be disgusted at Ferdinand\rquote s twisted view of what it is like to be a twin, but we can now understand from where it might have originated. \par
